{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And on the seventh day Elohim finished.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the intent of this verse is not that the Holy One, blessed be He, finished His work literally on the seventh day — for then the implication of this language would be that during part of the seventh day He was still doing work. Rather, the intent of the verse is that as soon as the seventh day arrived, He had already finished all His work. Similar to this is (Exodus 12:15): ״אך ביום הראשון תשביתו שאור״ — 'but on the first day you shall remove leaven' — where the intent is not to eliminate the leaven on the first day of Passover, but the intent is that when the first day of Passover arrives, the leaven must already be removed from your houses." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: ״ויכל אלהים ביום השביעי״ — R. Shimon said: flesh and blood, who does not know his times and moments, must add from the profane to the sacred; but the Holy One, blessed be He, who knows His times and moments, does not need to add from the profane to the sacred, but enters into it by a hair's breadth, and it appears as though on that very day He finished." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: ״ויכל אלהים ביום השביעי״ — this hints to you that all the works shall conclude in the seventh millennium, and this is what they said, blessed be their memory: 'and one [millennium] desolate.' ״וישבות ביום השביעי״ — the expression of שביתה applies to one who rests today and is destined to return to his work tomorrow; and here it hints that the work shall return after the seventh millennium. Therefore this passage of the seventh day was concluded with the word לעשות, to hint at work after the seventh day. And understand this."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:09:15.600393997Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}